\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Self-Consistency and extensions.}
SC selects the most frequent answer across chain-of-thought traces \citep{wang2023sc}. Extensions include universal SC for free-form generation \citep{chen2024usc}, confidence weighting \citep{taubenfeld2025cisc}, diverse prompting with step-aware verification \citep{li2023diverse}, and reasoning-aware filtering \citep{wan2024rasc, kim2026reasc}. \citet{loo2025reevaluating} show that SC gains plateau early. Our Condorcet analysis explains this: $\kappa = 0.21$--$0.82$ compresses $K_{\text{eff}}$ to 1.20--3.62 for $K = 12$ (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}).

\paragraph{Neuro-symbolic reasoning.}
Logic-LM \citep{pan2023logiclm} and LINC \citep{olausson2023linc} translate problems into FOL for external provers; LINC documents ${\sim}$40\% translation errors. Recent extensions---DyLoS \citep{xu2026dylos}, Aristotle \citep{li2025aristotle}, FoVer \citep{wang2025fover}, LogicAgent \citep{luo2025logicagent}---add solver selection, decomposition, trained verifiers, and multi-perspective grounding, each introducing capabilities beyond the reasoning model. \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show that LLM formalization degrades with complexity. Our oracle controls confirm the mechanism is sound ($+29.90$/$+14.22$\,pp FOLIO, $+11.50$\,pp ProofWriter); the failure localizes to self-extraction quality (\S\ref{sec:oracle}).

\paragraph{Test-time compute and verification.}
Scaling test-time computation through additional traces \citep{snell2025scaling} faces the same correlation challenge: same-model attempts collapse into correlated solutions. Reasoning models intensify this (QwQ-32B: $C_0 = 2.6\%$). Trained verifiers offer an alternative \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k, lightman2024prm, wang2024mathshepherd}; multi-agent debate refines answers through discussion \citep{liang2023encouraging, du2023improving}. Self-correction without external feedback degrades performance \citep{huang2024selfcorrect, stechly2025selfverification}, paralleling our confirmation bias finding (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}).

\paragraph{CJT and ensemble diversity.}
CJT \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees convergence under independent voting; \citet{ladha1992correlated} prove correlated voters reduce effective ensemble size; \citet{lefort2024condorcet_llm} apply CJT to LLM classification. We extend this by quantifying \emph{why} self-correction fails---same-model traces are correlated voters ($K_{\text{eff}}$ collapses)---and showing this persists even under formal verification (Z3-checked FOL) and at the process level (\S\ref{sec:process-kappa}).

\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

The analyses in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}--\S\ref{sec:remediation} confirm all three predictions of the Symbolic Mirage hypothesis (\S\ref{sec:mirage}): self-extracted constraints inherit the generating model's bias and cannot break the same-model ceiling. The oracle gap (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) confirms the aggregation mechanism is sound given uncontaminated constraints; the corrective signal must come from outside the model's distribution---escaping the mirage entirely.

\paragraph{Method.}
We test this with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M parameters, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}. For each problem, DeBERTa classifies the (premise, conclusion) pair into \textsc{entailment}, \textsc{contradiction}, or \textsc{neutral}. In combo mode, the NLI verdict is integrated with SC's $K$ votes via a weight parameter $w$: each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$).

\paragraph{Main result.}
DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo mode at $w{=}1$ (NLI as 13th voter) reaches 57.84\% ($\Delta = +1.96$\,pp, McNemar exact $p = 0.125$, n.s.; $b{=}0$, $c{=}4$); $w{=}3$ yields 58.82\% ($+2.94$\,pp, $p = 0.210$, n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n = 600$), $w{=}1$ yields $+0.83$\,pp (n.s.), $w{=}3$ achieves $+3.50$\,pp ($p = 0.013$), and $w{=}5$ reaches $+10.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$). Notably, same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter performance ($-2.83$\,pp, $p = 0.014$; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), while the cross-architecture combo yields significant improvement---illustrating the Symbolic Mirage prediction (\S\ref{sec:mirage}) that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution. We report $w{=}3$ on ProofWriter as the primary finding; higher weights risk overfitting. A per-label caveat: DeBERTa over-predicts \textsc{False} on ProofWriter (67.8\% of predictions) and performs poorly on \textsc{Unknown} (3.5\% accuracy), mirroring the FOLIO pattern where NLI's ``neutral'' fails to capture logical unknowability. This approach operates outside the same-model scope of SICA (\S\ref{sec:pipeline}).

\paragraph{Why it works.}
The 406M encoder-only DeBERTa and 7B decoder-only Mistral share no architecture, training data, or inference mechanism, so they err on different subsets---satisfying the independence condition that same-model sampling cannot provide (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). On FOLIO, the gains show per-label complementarity: NLI excels on \textsc{False} (71.43\% vs.\ SC's 38.10\%) via contradiction detection, while SC dominates \textsc{Unknown} (68.12\% vs.\ 18.84\%); when both agree, accuracy reaches 68.48\% ($+14.07$\,pp).

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Mechanism and regime structure.}
The Symbolic Mirage (\S\ref{sec:mirage}) manifests through two degradation channels: the \emph{direct} channel limits voting diversity (Eff-$K < 2$), while the \emph{indirect} channel causes MAX-SAT to degenerate to vote counting (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). The bias ratio gradient ($4.97\times$ Mistral $> 3.93\times$ Qwen $> 2.88\times$ LLaMA) aligns with regime boundaries: high bias ($4.24\times$ weighted) at intermediate SC produces SICA's only positive trends; low bias ($2.66\times$) at high SC yields the worst degradation ($-4.41$\,pp). Thinking-mode degeneration ($\kappa = 0.91$, Qwen3) confirms the trend continues with capability. For practitioners, $C_0$ combined with SC accuracy predicts when symbolic augmentation is unlikely to help. A methodological note: Z3 satisfiability is invariant to variable renaming (alpha-equivalence); the 56.4\% zero-variable-overlap rate between cross-answer constraint pairs (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}) reflects genuine semantic isolation, not a naming artifact.

\paragraph{Broader implications.}
The independence-accuracy trade-off instantiates a well-known tension in ensemble learning. \citet{krogh1994neural} decompose ensemble error into average individual error minus voter disagreement (the ``ambiguity'' term); \citet{brown2005diversity} formalize the principle that ensemble benefit requires both accuracy \emph{and} diversity. Our $K_{\text{eff}}$ framework provides an analogous decomposition for LLM traces: the Symbolic Mirage maximizes individual accuracy but collapses the ambiguity term, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} < 2$ despite nominal $K = 12$. CoT faithfulness compounds this: \citet{turpin2024language} show that chain-of-thought traces do not always reflect actual reasoning. If CoT is unfaithful to internal computation, then the mirage (Eq.~\ref{eq:mirage}) is an inherent property of the extraction process, not a correctable artifact---our process-level analysis, where cosine similarity ($0.69$--$0.79$) far exceeds answer-level $\kappa$ ($0.41$--$0.74$), corroborates this deeper homogeneity (\S\ref{sec:process-kappa}).

\paragraph{Conditions and open questions.}
The Symbolic Mirage provides a mechanistic explanation for why LLMs cannot reliably self-correct \citep{huang2024selfcorrect, stechly2025selfverification}: a $4.97\times$ bias ratio persists across fifteen remediation families. Three conditions may shift the frontier: (a)~70B+ models, though thinking-mode degeneration ($\kappa = 0.91$) suggests bias may intensify with scale; (b)~trained extractors decoupled from the reasoning model's distribution; and (c)~architecturally diverse verification (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

