\subsection{The Symbolic Mirage Hypothesis}
\label{sec:mirage}

We hypothesize that self-extracted symbolic structures are not independent verification signals but projections of the generating model's distributional biases. Formally, let $\mathcal{E}_\theta(t_i)$ denote constraints extracted by model $f_\theta$ from trace $t_i$ with answer $a_i$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mirage}
P\bigl(\mathcal{E}_\theta(t_i) \mid a^*, a_i\bigr) \neq P\bigl(\mathcal{E}_\theta(t_i) \mid a^*\bigr),
\end{equation}
i.e., extracted constraints depend on the model's answer $a_i$ given truth $a^*$. This \textbf{Symbolic Mirage} is specific to self-extracted constraints; symbolic methods employing independently sourced constraints remain effective (\S\ref{sec:oracle}). The mirage degrades aggregation through two channels: correlated constraints compress $K_{\text{eff}} < 2$ (\emph{direct}; Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}), and answer-coupled constraints collapse MAX-SAT to vote counting (\emph{indirect}; Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). The bias ratio $\text{BR} = P(\text{support} \mid \text{wrong}) / P(\text{support} \mid \text{correct})$ quantifies mirage intensity. Three testable predictions follow: (1)~BR $> 1.0$ across architectures (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}); (2)~no same-model intervention reduces BR below 1.0 (\S\ref{sec:remediation}); (3)~only external verification escapes the mirage (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

